(registered 2026-11-18, last updated 2026-11-18)

Media type name: application

Media subtype name: prs.zbook

Required parameters: N/A

Optional parameters: N/A

Encoding considerations: binary

Security considerations: The .zbook media type relies on a declarative, plain-text structure formatted using standard JSON nodes as specified in RFC 8259. It contains no active or executable content, scripts, macros, or system binaries. The textual fields are intended to be parsed strictly by standard JSON parsers and Markdown display engines, making it inherently safe from local runtime execution vulnerabilities. The format does not natively provide encryption or integrity services; data privacy and integrity must be handled externally using standard transport security protocols like TLS/HTTPS.

Interoperability considerations: The .zbook format is fully compliant with the standard JSON specifications detailed in IETF RFC 8259. It uses standard text-based Markdown payloads within structured object nodes, allowing any system, web browser, or e-reading application with a standard JSON parser to read, extract, and render the underlying book chapters across all operating systems without compatibility errors.

Published Specification:
https://sp20101810-dot.github.io/zbook/SPECIFICATION-DRAFT-0.1.md

GitHub Repository:
https://github.com/sp20101810-dot/zbook

The specification describes the ZBOOK file format, its structure, required metadata, and technical design principles.

Applications which use this media: Custom e-reading software suites, digital literature archive systems, independent content management platforms, and structured Markdown text editors.

Fragment identifier considerations: None. The .zbook media type does not currently define any media-type-specific fragment identifiers.

Restrictions on usage: None. This media type is free from restriction and can be used openly across any compliant software system.

